After physical installation and switch configuration, cluster-management software provisions the machines. Provisioning gives servers the intended operating system, drivers, network settings and services. The scheduler then decides which machines run a job, while monitoring records health and performance.

Configuration consistency matters because distributed work assumes its participants follow compatible rules. One mismatched driver or network setting can leave healthy hardware waiting. Inventory records must also match physical reality: the operator needs to know which server, cable, switch port and cooling connection produced an alert.

NVIDIA's deployment documentation validates the completed pod against its expected configuration. Its administration guidance also recommends both single-node and multi-node tests. A local diagnostic can prove that one server's GPUs are healthy under stress. A collective communication test checks whether many servers exchange data consistently. Storage and representative application tests reveal limits that component checks miss.

Follow a ladder of evidence

Return to the illustrative 32-server cluster. The team can move upward in controlled steps:

1. Verify each server, switch, power path and cooling connection.
2. Confirm every server receives the same approved software configuration.
3. Test small groups to isolate cabling, network or storage problems.
4. Run the full 32-server communication and application workload repeatedly.
5. Simulate the failures the design promises to tolerate, then verify alarms, failover and recovery.

The order matters. Jumping directly to a large job creates many possible causes. Stopping after individual tests misses interactions that appear only when the cluster is busy.

Prediction check

All 32 servers pass their local health checks, and four separate eight-server jobs run normally. The full 32-server job is much slower than expected. Is the cluster accepted for that workload?

Answer: No. The smaller tests show that the servers can work, but they do not prove that shared network or storage paths can support the full group. The tempting mistake is to add successful component tests and call the combined system successful.

Installed capacity is not yet productive capacity

For an operator, the handoff should include test results, baselines, equipment records, alarm procedures and named responsibility for unresolved defects. Those records make later changes diagnosable: if performance falls, the team can compare it with a known-good starting point.

For an investor, commissioning explains why reported chip deliveries, powered buildings and revenue-producing compute can occur at different times. Integrators, test tools and service providers can be important because they shorten that gap. Demand does not guarantee attractive profits; standardization, labor intensity, warranty risk, competition and customer bargaining determine who captures value.

A component is delivered when it arrives. A system is usable when representative jobs complete predictably and the promised failure cases have been tested.

